<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Greater Republic of Central America</h1><p class="st-meta">1896 CE – 1898 CE · capital Amapala</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Union of Honduras, Nicaragua and El Salvador formed in 1896, one of several failed attempts to revive the Central American federation.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The liberal presidents <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/policarpo-bonilla">Policarpo Bonilla</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-honduras">Honduras</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-santos-zelaya">José Santos Zelaya</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-nicaragua">Nicaragua</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rafael-antonio-gutierrez">Rafael Antonio Gutiérrez</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-el-salvador">El Salvador</a> signed the Pact of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amapala">Amapala</a> in June 1896, creating the Greater Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a>. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-guatemala">Guatemala</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/republic-of-costa-rica">Costa Rica</a> were invited but stayed out.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The union was a confederation in which each state kept its internal government. A Diet of delegates, meeting at the Honduran island port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amapala">Amapala</a>, handled foreign affairs and was recognised by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a>. In 1898 a constitution converted it into a federation called the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a>, due to take effect on 1 November 1898.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Within days, General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tomas-regalado">Tomás Regalado</a> seized power in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-el-salvador">El Salvador</a> and withdrew it from the union, which then dissolved in late 1898. The episode showed how fragile the unionist ideal had become; another attempt followed with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/federation-of-central-america-1921-1922">Federation of Central America</a> in 1921.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/greater-republic-of-central-america.jpg" alt="Greater Republic of Central America" width="320" height="193"><figcaption>Image: No machine-readable author provided. Fornax assumed (based on copyright claims). · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Greater_Republic_of_Central_America_(1898).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1896 CE – 1898 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Amapala</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Policarpo Bonilla, José Santos Zelaya, Rafael Antonio Gutiérrez</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Spanish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/honduras">Honduras</a>, <a href="/countries/el-salvador">El Salvador</a>, <a href="/countries/nicaragua">Nicaragua</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>República Mayor de Centroamérica, Republic of Central America</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1897&amp;focus=greater-republic-of-central-america" aria-label="Greater Republic of Central America on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2276 1682 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Greater Republic of Central America lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M2596 1938L2556 1924M2596 1938L2626 1889M2596 1938L2668 1949"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M2556 1924h0M2626 1889h0M2668 1949h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2596 1938h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2596 1938h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Amapala. <a href="/atlas?year=1897&amp;focus=greater-republic-of-central-america">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

